      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Ailey officially incorporated as a town in 1893, just two years after its post office opened in 1891.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ailey%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The historic Rosenwald School sits beside Ailey’s oldest African‑American church, a reminder of the town’s civil‑rights legacy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://vanishinggeorgia.com/category/ailey-ga/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ailey’s former railroad depot was restored in 1999 and now serves as the town’s City Hall, preserving its rail heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://vanishinggeorgia.com/category/ailey-ga/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Nepsey Methodist Church, founded in 1877 and named for Sister Nepsey Johnson, remains a cornerstone of Ailey’s spiritual life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://vanishinggeorgia.com/category/ailey-ga/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "U.S. Route 280 cuts through Ailey, linking the town westward to Mount Vernon and eastward to Vidalia.",
